        explanation: "After Cleopatra's suicide on August 12, 30 BCE, the seventeen-year-old Caesarion was on the Red Sea coast preparing to sail for India with treasure and a tutor named Rhodon. Octavian's agents bribed or blackmailed Rhodon to convince Caesarion that Octavian would let him return as a Roman client king of Egypt. Caesarion turned back. Once in Octavian's hands in Alexandria, he was strangled within days. His body was never publicly displayed and his burial site is unknown. Octavian's philosophical adviser Areius justified the murder by quoting Homer's Iliad: 'It is not good to have too many Caesars.' Months later, the Senate granted Octavian the title 'Augustus.'",
